    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">6th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The first city of Merv is founded as part of the Achaemenid expansion under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">3rd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Seleucid ruler Antiochus Soter renames the city Antiochia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a> and rebuilds it at Gyaur Gala</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">3rd century</span><span class="rf-text">The Sasanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> takes Merv, beginning almost four centuries of Sasanian rule</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">671</span><span class="rf-text">Ziyad ibn Abi Sufyan sends 50,000 Arab troops to Merv as a garrison</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">748</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a> declares the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid dynasty</a> at Merv</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1037</span><span class="rf-text">The Seljuq <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a> take over Merv peacefully</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1221</span><span class="rf-text">Merv opens its gates to Tolui, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, and most of its people are killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1789</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-murad">Shah Murad of Bukhara</a> razes the city and deports its population</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="Darius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rumlu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Merv lay in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-niger-delta">inland delta</a> of the Murghab river, which flows north from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> into the Karakum Desert. Archaeological surveys have found traces of village life on the site as far back as the 3rd millennium BCE and have linked the area culturally with the Bactria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a> Archaeological Complex. The delta, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called Margiana, gave the place a water supply in the midst of dry land and a position on the routes from north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, and at the junction of the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> with the route from Tus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> Merv, under the name Mouru, was one of sixteen perfect lands created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a>, and the geography of the Zend-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avesta">Avesta</a> mentions it beside Balkh. Under the name Margu it appears as part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> in the Behistun inscriptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>, of about 515 BCE. The first city of Merv was founded in the 6th century BCE as part of the Achaemenid expansion under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>, though later strata cover the Achaemenid levels deeply. The oldest part of the city complex, Erk Gala, was a fortress in the Persian style controlling the oasis; it later served as the acropolis of the Hellenistic city and the citadel of the Islamic one.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-muslim.jpg" alt="Abu Muslim" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Abu_Muslim_(d._755)_from_the_genealogy_(silsilan%C4%81ma),_Cream_of_Histories_(Z%C3%BCbdet-%C3%BCt_Tevarih,_1598).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1598 author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the Seleucid ruler Antiochus Soter, who reigned from 281 to 261 BCE, renamed the city Antiochia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a>, he rebuilt and expanded it at the site now known as Gyaur Gala. Its walls there, built one on top of another, are the largest pre-Islamic fortifications found in the Garagum, and a continuous series of Sasanian coins minted at Merv documents almost four centuries of direct Sasanian rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a> seeds from levels as far back as the 5th century CE are the first sign that cotton textiles were already important to the Sasanian city.</p><p>Merv gained new importance under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a> expanded it, and the walled city of Soltangala, built to the west of Gyaur Gala, became the core of medieval Merv, while Gyaur Gala remained a craftsmen's quarter. Throughout the Abbasid era Merv was the capital and most important city of Khurasan. Under the Seljuqs it reached its greatest size. Arab and Persian geographers called it the mother of the world, the chief city of Khurasan and the capital of the eastern Islamic world. According to Tertius Chandler it was the largest city in the world by 1150, with 200,000 people, and by 1210 it may have had as many as 500,000.</p><p>Merv was also known for its textiles: the 12th-century geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">al-Idrisi</a> wrote of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and of Merv cotton, which he called extremely soft.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ardashir-i.jpg" alt="Ardashir I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_of_Ardashir_I_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Merv was the centre of the Achaemenid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a>. After Alexander's death in 323 BCE it became the capital of the province of Margiana under the Seleucid, Greco-Bactrian, Parthian and Sasanian states. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> and the Kushans took control in succession after the fall of the Seleucid dynasty. The Sasanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> took Merv, and Sasanian rule, interrupted briefly by a Hephthalite occupation from the end of the 5th century to 565 CE, lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a> was killed near the city in 651 and the Sasanian military governor surrendered to the Arab army.</p><p>Representatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Umar occupied the city, which became the capital of the Umayyad province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. From Merv, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> led the Arab conquest of large parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> between 705 and 715. In February 748 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a> declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid dynasty</a> there, and he ruled Merv as a semi-independent prince until his assassination. Between 813 and 818 the caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> lived in Merv, which effectively made it the capital of the Muslim world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirids</a> replaced Arab rule in 821 and ruled until 873, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> and the Ghaznavids.</p><p>In 1037 the Seljuqs under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a> took the city peacefully. Merv became the eastern capital of the divided Seljuq state and from 1118 the capital of the whole empire under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-sanjar">Ahmad Sanjar</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> nomads pillaged it in 1153, and it passed to the Ghurids in 1192 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmians">Khwarazmians</a> in 1204.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Merv&#8221; cites where it covers Hellenistic era, Parthian era, Sasanian era, Arab conquest and influence:</p><ul><li>Georgina Herrmann, Monuments of Merv: Traditional Buildings of the Karakum (Society of Antiquaries of London, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Merv was a city of many faiths. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>, beside the official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, it held Buddhists, Manichaeans and Christians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>. It was a major centre of Buddhist learning, with monasteries and stupas at Gyaur Kala and Baýramaly, and between the 5th and 11th centuries it was the seat of an East Syrian metropolitan province. After the Arab conquest Merv and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> became one of the first parts of the Persian-speaking world to be majority Muslim, and Arab immigration was substantial; the Chinese visitor Du Huan observed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> lived there in mixed concentrations. A substantial Christian community remained, and in 1009 the Archbishop of Merv wrote to the Patriarch at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> about the fasting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a>. Ali ar-Ridha, the 8th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> of Twelver Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, moved to Merv and lived there for several years, and al-Muqanna, the Veiled Prophet, who claimed to be an incarnation of God, began his movement there.</p><p>Merv was one of the great cities of Muslim scholarship, with Samarqand and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. It produced scholars of law, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>, history and literature, among them many called Marwazi, and the historian Yaqut studied in its libraries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqut-al-hamawi">Yaqut al-Hamawi</a> counted as many as ten large libraries, one of them in a major mosque with 12,000 volumes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-khayyam">Omar Khayyam</a> spent several years working at its observatory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a> founded a library and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a>. The oasis was watered by canals cut from the Murghab. A workshop of the 9th or 10th century excavated in the 1990s produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crucible-steel">crucible steel</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="Genghis Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1153 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> nomads from beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> pillaged Merv at the end of Sanjar's rule. The greatest disaster came in 1221, when the city opened its gates to Tolui, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. Most of the inhabitants are said to have been killed. The Arab historian Ibn al-Athir, writing from the accounts of refugees from Merv, described the executions and the torture of the wealthy, the burning of the city and the burning of Sanjar's tomb, and put the dead at around 700,000. The Persian historian Juvayni gave more than 1,300,000. Almost the whole population, together with refugees from other parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, was killed. Its walls had held off at least one earlier Mongol offensive before it fell.</p><p>Excavations show that the fortifications were rebuilt after their destruction, but the city's prosperity had passed. Merv became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and was looted repeatedly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>. Early in the 14th century it was the seat of a Christian archbishopric under the Kartids, and by 1380 it belonged to the empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> occupied Merv in 1505, and Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> expelled them five years later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-khan-ii">Abdullah Khan II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> conquered it in 1593, and Shah Abbas soon took it. In 1785 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-murad">Shah Murad</a>, the Manghit amir of Bukhara, attacked the city and killed its ruler Bayram 'Ali Khan Qajar. In 1788 and 1789 he razed it to the ground and broke down the dams. About 100,000 people of the city and oasis were deported to the Bukharan oasis and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> region. By 1888 the city was entirely abandoned.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-curzon.jpg" alt="George Curzon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">George Curzon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:George_Curzon2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Grantham Bain Collection (Library of Congress), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Merv's architecture is said to have inspired the Abbasid re-planning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and the geographers al-Muqaddasi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a> wrote of its palaces, groves, gardens and streams. The deportees of 1788 and 1789 were the last Persian-speaking Shias of Merv. Their descendants, known as Marvis, live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and the area between, and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> listed them as Iranians through the 1980s. They have kept their Shia religion and their Mervi identity and are counted apart from the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a>.</p><p>The site consists of several walled cities built close together in different periods and then abandoned: Erk Gala, Gyaur Gala, Soltangala and the later Timurid Abdyllahangala. The best preserved building is the 12th-century mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Sanjar, the largest of the Seljuq mausolea and the first dated mosque-mausoleum complex. It is square, 27 metres to a side, and its turquoise dome could be seen, it was said, by caravans a day's march away. Among the remains of Abbasid Merv are the köshks, two-storey semi-fortified palaces of a type unique to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in that period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">George Curzon</a>, who visited in 1888, wrote of walls, towers, ramparts and domes stretching to the horizon. Excavations began in 1885, and since 1992 the Ancient Merv Project has worked there, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> authorities. The site lies north of the village of Baýramaly, 30 km east of Mary, in a park established in 1987, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> lists it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Merv was an Iranian oasis city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, founded in the 6th century BCE, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid dynasty</a> was declared in 748 and which became the Seljuq capital. It was largely destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1221 and razed in 1788 and 1789. Its ruins are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>.</em></p>

